WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.898
The incident response plan is the umbrella document for the response program.

2
00:00:05.018 --> 00:00:13.998
It defines authority, scope, roles, severity model, communications expectations, escalation paths, legal and privacy considerations, and the broad lifecycle the organization follows.

3
00:00:14.118 --> 00:00:19.016
A playbook takes that plan and applies it to a specific scenario.

4
00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:26.891
The plan says how the organization responds. The playbook says how to respond when this type of thing happens.

5
00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:35.174
Most incident response programs use a lifecycle that includes preparation, detection and analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, and post-incident improvement.

6
00:00:35.294 --> 00:00:44.682
The names may vary, but the flow is familiar. Preparation builds the people, tools, access, logging, contacts, and documentation needed before an incident.

7
00:00:44.802 --> 00:00:50.108
Detection and analysis turn signals into an understanding of what may be happening.

8
00:00:50.228 --> 00:00:55.534
Containment limits damage. Eradication removes the cause, foothold, malicious artifact, or unauthorized access.

9
00:00:55.654 --> 00:01:01.368
Recovery restores normal operation safely. Post-incident improvement captures lessons learned and updates controls.

10
00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:10.468
A playbook makes those stages concrete. In preparation, it lists required data sources, access prerequisites, contact groups, ticket templates, and known dependencies.

11
00:01:10.588 --> 00:01:26.914
In detection and analysis, it defines triggering conditions and triage questions. In containment, it lists options such as isolating a host, disabling an account, revoking tokens, blocking an indicator, quarantining email, restricting a network path, or pausing an exposed service.

12
00:01:27.034 --> 00:01:35.606
In eradication and recovery, it describes how to remove the unwanted condition and verify that the system is safe to return.

13
00:01:35.726 --> 00:01:42.664
In post-incident improvement, it points to closure criteria, lessons learned, control updates, and any required reporting.

14
00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:48.091
The playbook should also identify owners. During an incident, vague ownership wastes time.

15
00:01:48.211 --> 00:01:52.292
The SOC may own triage. IT may own endpoint actions.

16
00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:59.351
The identity team may own account controls. The cloud team may own audit logs and key revocation.

17
00:01:59.471 --> 00:02:05.185
Legal or privacy teams may own notification analysis. Business owners may decide acceptable downtime.

18
00:02:05.305 --> 00:02:09.795
Communications may prepare stakeholder updates. Executives may need concise risk summaries.

19
00:02:09.915 --> 00:02:19.303
The playbook does not need every person's name forever, but it should define the role or group responsible for each decision and action.

20
00:02:19.423 --> 00:02:25.953
Alignment matters. A playbook should fit the organization's escalation model, legal obligations, communications process, and tooling.

21
00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:33.829
If the incident response plan requires legal review before external notifications, the playbook should not invent a separate path.

22
00:02:33.949 --> 00:02:41.296
If the organization uses a ticketing system as the record of action, the playbook should name that record.

23
00:02:41.416 --> 00:02:50.803
If cloud audit logs are retained in a central system, the playbook should point responders there rather than making them search from scratch.

24
00:02:50.923 --> 00:02:57.046
Think of the plan as the governance layer and the playbook as the operational layer.

25
00:02:57.166 --> 00:03:05.737
The playbook translates policy and lifecycle language into responder behavior: trigger, triage, classify, contain, eradicate, recover, document, communicate, close, and improve.
